Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: “Route Seven Crosses the Fourth Bar”
- Story & Context: At 112 BPM in 4/4, Lucía begins a seven-string ascending harp route built from tonic, fifth, suspended second, sixth, octave, seventh, and upper fifth. She repeats the route without resetting at barlines. Evan plays a two-bar clean guitar pattern that never follows her phrase ending. Mateo keeps a compact kick and caja figure centered on the four-beat frame. Camila remains silent until the first major Alignment Point, where all four players strike one dry suspended chord together. Naomi enters afterward with one long bass-flute fourth, temporarily smoothing the geometry before ROUTE 7 resumes. The final section reverses the same seven pitches downward while the rhythm section remains identical.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Large graph-paper river chart with seven diagonal string lines crossing four vertical bar divisions, one intersection circled in red, natural harp wood and brass survey dividers in shallow focus. Sparse lyrical fragments limited to “seven across,” “four below,” “meet on one,” and “do not restart.”

Track 2: “Gauge House Counts Nine Without Moving”
- Story & Context: At 121 BPM, the ensemble alternates 7/8 and 9/8 while Lucía maintains one nine-string descending route for almost the entire piece. The changing bar lengths cause the apparent accent of the harp to migrate unpredictably even though her hand pattern never changes. Evan uses clipped add9 guitar dyads on the starts of the written measures, deliberately exposing the difference between meter and route. Mateo accents groups of 3+2+2 and 2+2+2+3 without adding fills. Camila uses dead-stroke vibraphone notes only where the nine-string route crosses a bar start. Naomi appears in a middle section with an unmetered low-air phrase over sustained harp harmonics before the counting architecture returns.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Abandoned river-gauge cabinet containing nine handwritten level marks while two transparent timing grids of seven and nine divisions overlap behind it. Sparse vocal fragments limited to “gauge nine,” “same hand,” “bar changed,” and “water does not count.”

Track 3: “The Bridge Span Meets Eleven at Dusk”
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 98 BPM and uses the EP's longest route: eleven adjacent strings moving low to high against alternating 5/8 + 4/8 bars. Lucía plays softly at first while Evan uses isolated baritone-guitar harmonics and open fifths. Mateo begins with caja rim clicks only. Camila marks every second Alignment Point with a prepared-vibraphone octave. As the piece develops, Lucía moves the same eleven-count route one register higher while the ensemble gradually adds weight without increasing tempo. The final Alignment Point arrives after a long sequence of near-misses; all players land on one dry unison, then stop immediately. Naomi alone sustains a low bass-flute note into the room after everyone else cuts.
